From 062d378ffe2e135e4f5aa23d9283b532e88a794f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 06:07:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] gles: the GUI in HDR with the tone curve of a TV in SDR
 (BT.1886), as ITU-R BT.2408 maps SDR

Over HDR10, HLG and Dolby Vision (native, the menus in Dolby Vision, shown as HDR10 or HDR10+), the
GUI, the OSD and text and SDR image subtitles are made PQ or HLG by the GUI composite, which took their
light by the sRGB EOTF of IEC 61966-2-1, as Kodi's composite does. The straight segment at the bottom
of that curve makes the darkest greys far lighter than an SDR display shows the same GUI: a TV made to
the reference decodes the SDR signal by BT.1886 (a 2.4 power, with an OLED's black at zero), and the
reference display that the sRGB encoding was fitted to has a 2.2 power. Measured on a software renderer
with the shader of the tree at the reference white (203 cd/m2), against BT.1886: a 2 % grey at 0.308
cd/m2 instead of 0.016 (Delta E ITP 51), a 10 % grey at 2.10 instead of 0.85 (35.6), Estuary's dark
grey (28, 32, 40) at 2.91 instead of 1.38 (31.9), a 50 % grey 8.1, the primaries and secondaries at
75 % 2.5 to 3.0; white and the full primaries the same. Against a 2.2 power: up to 42 in the darkest
greys.

ITU-R BT.2408 (5.1) maps SDR into PQ or HLG display-referred "when the goal is to preserve the colours
and relative tones seen on an SDR display": the BT.1886 EOTF, BT.709 to BT.2020 as ITU-R BT.2087 gives
it, and 100 % SDR at the HDR reference white of 203 cd/m2, which is also its graphics white (2.1). The
composite already had the matrix and the white; it now has the curve too, as a setting at the expert
level beside the GUI white (videoscreen.hadesguicurve, "GUI and subtitle gamma in HDR"): BT.1886 by
default, 2.2 for a TV or a monitor set to it, and Kodi's sRGB, which gives Kodi's output to the bit.
Nothing else changes: the video, image subtitles made for HDR, white and every colour at full strength.

The exact conversion (highp), the LUTs of a GPU without highp and the GUI read in place by the packer
of native Dolby Vision output (GUI_COMPOSITE of dvbridge_source.c, gui_gamma) take the same curve; the
packer is not asked to composite in place with a curve it does not take. A change of the setting
applies from the next frame, as a change of the white does, and is a new key of the composite for the
GUI cache. kodi.log tells what the GUI goes out as when that changes (PQ or HLG, its white, its curve),
and the line of the menus in Dolby Vision names the curve.

Measured on zink over lavapipe with the shaders of the tree: every code 0..255 of a grey, a red, a
green and a blue ramp and greys over 128 levels of coverage, at GUI whites of 25, 100 and 300 %, in PQ
and HLG, for each curve: Kodi's composite within 0.034 codes of 4095 of the conversion in double
precision, the packer's within 0.034, and every pixel that the GUI does not cover keeps its bits. With
the shaders before this change the same test fails for BT.1886 (293 codes off at 100 %, 411 at 300 %)
and for 2.2 (240 at 100 %); the sRGB curve gives the same values as before. Seven mutations of the
composite and two of the packer's are caught.
